  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 147 AM EDT Sat Sep 5 2026\n\nIn the upper levels, the trough dips into the Ohio Valley as the \nridge narrows to the west centered over the Great Plains. At the \nsurface, a weak cold front will move through the region this \nevening, bringing a better chance for showers and storms especially \nfrom Knoxville northward. With better synoptic lift in place today, \nthere is a higher chance for strong to severe storms especially in \nnortheast Tennessee and southwest Virginia (SPC Slight risk), closer \nto the trough. The rest of the region is under a marginal risk for \nsevere. Storm activity will likely be more isolated from around \nKnoxville southward. Guidance is starting to hint at a minor short \nwave or two moving through the pattern from Kentucky into East \nTennessee. HRRR is picking up on an organized cluster moving down \nfrom Kentucky possibly in the early afternoon hours. With good \ninstability (about 2500 J/kg) but weak deep layer shear and high \nfreezing levels, the primary threat will be damaging straight-line \nwinds. Hail less than one inch will be possible in the strongest \nstorms. It is hard to nail down the frontal timing since the front \nis weak but I am thinking it will be more in the evening or \novernight for actual frontal passage. An organized cluster of storms \nor MCS moving down from Kentucky will be possible this afternoon \nthrough late evening. The CAMs have a couple of waves of storms \ncoming down from Kentucky one in the early afternoon and one in the \nevening, generally weakening as they move south.\n\nSlightly cooler temps expected by Sunday after frontal passage. \nAlthough, not so much near Chattanooga despite what appears to be a \nclean frontal passage. There will still be some showers and storms \nmainly in the easternmost counties Sunday as the region stays \nsandwiched between a ridge to the west and a trough to the east. \nMonday will have cooler temps (although still very warm) with highs \nin the Tennessee Valley mainly in the upper 80s. \n\nBy Monday and especially Tuesday the ridge will drift back into the \nSoutheast, bringing hot, dry conditions through at least midweek. It \ndoesn't look quite as hot as this week was, highs will likely be in \nthe lower 90s for the Central and Southern Tennessee Valley opposed \nto the upper 90s from this past week.\n\nDaily Record High Maximum Temperatures\n\nDate    Chattanooga    Knoxville      Tri-Cities     Oak Ridge \n      \n09-05   104(1925)      103(1954)      99(1954)       102(1954)",
